  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A rise in escalation risk prompts governments to impose formal or informal limits on crude and refined exports to deny supplies to adversaries and conserve domestic fuel stocks.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">indo_pacific_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_fuel_supply_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="indo_pacific_rivalry::crisis_escalation_risk" target="global_fuel_supply_shock::refined_inventory_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Heightened likelihood of conflict leads governments and commercial buyers to accelerate on‑hand stockpiling of refined transport fuels as an immediate buffer against anticipated disruptions.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">indo_pacific_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">global_fuel_supply_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="indo_pacific_rivalry::crisis_escalation_risk" target="hormuz_redsea_tensions::tanker_security_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">Heightened Indo‑Pacific crisis risk raises great‑power distraction and risk tolerance among regional proxies and state actors, who exploit security gaps or test resolve by increasing maritime harassment and attacks on tankers in the Gulf and Red Sea.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">indo_pacific_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">hormuz_redsea_tensions</data>
</edge>
<edge source="indo_pacific_rivalry::crisis_escalation_risk" target="russia_ukraine_war::kinetic_attack_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">An acute uptick in Indo‑Pacific crisis risk prompts rapid redeployment of Western naval/air assets and political attention away from Europe, reducing immediate deterrence and creating windows that enable Moscow to increase the tempo or scale of kinetic operations in Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">indo_pacific_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
